#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100650 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39738d1a-2b58-58e7-8b93-fae76501045c" :knowledge-point/title "Minimizing the Surface Area of a Cylinder With a Fixed Volume"}] :question/id #uuid "c7f66eb5-8151-5886-9b35-58632319651f" :question/math-academy-id "e-351" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base but no lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $27\\pi\\, \\textrm{in}^3$. What is the radius of the cylinder that gives the smallest surface area?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/ad/adb9c56132e8f346a07a9a644cd364c2347ad8c551a3c9bd4842b7067eb1d050.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS= 2\\pi r h + \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $27\\pi \\, \\textrm{in}^3$, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n27\\pi = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh = \\dfrac{27}{r^2}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} S(r)& = 2\\pi r \\left(\\dfrac{27}{r^2}\\right) + \\pi r^2 \\\\ & = \\dfrac{54\\pi}{r} + \\pi r^2. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S$, so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\nS'(r) = -\\dfrac{54\\pi}{r^2} + 2\\pi r\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(r) &= 0 \\\\\n- \\frac{54π}{r^{2}} + 2πr &= 0 \\\\\n2πr &= \\frac{54π}{r^{2}} \\\\\n2πr^{3} &= 54π \\\\\nr^{3} &= 27 \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\\nr &= 3in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r < 3$ then $S'(r) < 0$, and if $r > 3$ then $S'(r) > 0$, which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r)$.\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius\n\n$$\nr =3\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100651 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fae559-f88f-50ec-a1f0-a7cfeb319d19" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Volume of a Cylinder With a Fixed Surface Area"}] :question/id #uuid "c82b0d87-792b-57d2-9340-9095af643c44" :question/math-academy-id "e-352" :question/problem "We need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $25\\pi\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?\n\n![](images/a2/a2916167b3c8d4169339280fe67a0018b5d09a9784260cf6488cd6825753b2b9.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $25\\pi\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}$, so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi r h + \\pi r^2 = 25\\pi\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2πrh &= 25π - πr^{2} \\\\\nh &= \\frac{25π - πr^{2}}{2πr} \\\\\nh &= \\frac{25 - r^{2}}{2r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(r) &= πr^{2} \\cdot \\frac{25 - r^{2}}{2r} \\\\\nV(r) &= ((25r - r^{3})π)/(2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V$, so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\nV'(r) = \\frac{(25 - 3 r^2)\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV^{′}(r) &= 0 \\\\\n((25 - 3r^{2})π)/(2) &= 0 \\\\\n25 &= 3r^{2} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{25}{3} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3}cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root in the last step because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nis a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nV''(r) = -3\\pi r\n$$\n\nand we can see that\n\n$$\nV''\\left(\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right) < 0\n$$\n\nSo, the stationary point\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nis indeed a maximum of $V(r)$.\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\, \\textrm{cm}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100783 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8ac7b46-da1d-57f9-ab61-efa672ca8128" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing the Cost of Building a Cylinder"}] :question/id #uuid "3d2657d6-3dc6-5855-8464-4f23dc675211" :question/math-academy-id "e-7523" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical tank with a base and a lid. The tank must have an exact volume of $18\\pi\\,\\mathrm{m}^3$.\nTo make the tank, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a metal sheet that costs $\\[math]1.50$ per $\\mathrm{m}^2$ for the lateral surface,\n- a metal sheet that costs $\\[/math]0.50$ per $\\mathrm{m}^2$ for the base and lid.\n\nWhat is the radius of the tank that gives the smallest possible manufacturing cost?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/d7/d72b9a87e460f841bbd135fd16806607c16aa0b37b2279727bd7d9eeeb9c6982.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 2πrh \\cdot 1.5 + 2πr^{2} \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 3πrh + πr^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $18\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^3$, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n18\\pi = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh = \\dfrac{18}{r^2}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(r) &= 3πr(\\frac{18}{r^{2}}) + πr^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{54π}{r} + πr^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C$, so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\nC'(r) = -\\dfrac{54\\pi}{r^2} + 2\\pi r\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC^{′}(r) &= 0 \\\\\n- \\frac{54π}{r^{2}} + 2πr &= 0 \\\\\n2πr &= \\frac{54π}{r^{2}} \\\\\n2πr^{3} &= 54π \\\\\nr^{3} &= 27 \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\\nr &= 3m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r < 3$ then $C'(r) < 0$, and if $r > 3$ then $C'(r) > 0$, which means that the stationary point $r = 3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r)$.\n\nTherefore, the tank with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius\n\n$$\nr = 3\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fae559-f88f-50ec-a1f0-a7cfeb319d19" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Volume of a Cylinder With a Fixed Surface Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84537527-8aad-5079-96d5-367c58417266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72πcm^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098984} {:answer/id #uuid "bb349210-57fd-5a97-ade4-91b18e7bd0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58πcm^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098985} {:answer/id #uuid "c747ebc0-007f-588b-bd5f-bba27ed13dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46πcm^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098986} {:answer/id #uuid "10beb0e4-5b03-534d-b076-f843369f0e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64πcm^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098987} {:answer/id #uuid "5416a513-8bd9-5615-a645-5fe545fd93fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} 